WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.720
Source ingestion is the first architectural control point in private RAG.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.260
The system may ingest files, wikis, knowledge bases, tickets, source repositories,

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.455
policy libraries, transcripts, shared drives, structured records,

00:00:11.455 --> 00:00:12.830
or other internal sources.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.020
Each source arrives with its own owner, permission model, quality level, update pattern,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.190
retention expectation, and sensitivity.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:23.300
A private RAG pipeline should not flatten those differences into one uncontrolled pile of

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:23.720
text.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.730
During ingestion, the system should preserve the source system, document owner,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.880
classification, access rules, ingestion date, modified date, retention status,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.240
lifecycle state, and deletion expectations.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.700
Those details are not paperwork.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.265
They are the data controls that later retrieval depends on.

00:00:38.265 --> 00:00:40.535
Boundaries also exist inside content.

00:00:40.535 --> 00:00:45.130
A document may contain sections, tables, appendices, exceptions, regional instructions,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.675
tenant-specific clauses, or archived material.

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:51.085
A shared repository may mix public guidance with restricted notes.

00:00:51.085 --> 00:00:53.880
A policy library may contain current and retired versions.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.530
Ingestion should keep those distinctions visible enough for chunking, filtering,

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:58.060
and review.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.760
Data normalization is useful when it makes content searchable and consistent.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.070
Boundary loss is dangerous when it removes ownership, classification,

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.125
or the meaning of where a statement came from.

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:10.585
The goal is to prepare content for retrieval without stripping away the rules that made

00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:12.050
the original source governable.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.308
Private RAG starts by respecting source systems.

00:01:14.308 --> 00:01:18.230
If a document is expired, confidential, tenant-specific, archived,

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.480
or restricted before ingestion, it should remain recognizable as such after ingestion.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.460
Carrying that meaning forward makes retrieval safer, review easier,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.330
and deletion workflows more reliable.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.185
Treat ingestion as a governed handoff.

00:01:29.185 --> 00:01:32.760
Before content enters the RAG pipeline, the team should know who approved it,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.440
what connector touched it, what transformations occurred,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.720
and what evidence would be needed to remove or correct it later.
